<Read
  slug="the-model-comes-home"
  description={"Canada invented the way the world's retirement savings own roads, ports and power, and its pension funds own them in Britain, Australia and Chile. At home they hold about 7% of their infrastructure, while the water under Canadian streets needs renewing faster than it is renewed. How the model comes home, with the assets kept public."}
  standfirst={"Canada built the model the world copies for owning roads, ports and power with retirement savings. Its funds own airports abroad. Under our own streets the pipes are wearing out. Here is how the model comes home, and the water stays public."}
>
      <section class="brief" aria-labelledby="in-brief">
        <h2 id="in-brief">In brief</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>Canada built the pension model the world studies: arm’s-length boards, in-house teams, and direct ownership of real assets. Eight funds now hold about $2.6 trillion.</li>
          <li>They own ports in Britain and Australia, airports across Europe, power lines and toll roads. By one study, Canadian funds keep about 7% of their infrastructure at home. Australian funds keep 46%.</li>
          <li>Under Canadian streets, $124.3 billion of municipal water, wastewater and stormwater assets were in poor or very poor condition at the end of 2024, up from 2022. Across all core public infrastructure, owners spent about a ninth of what renewal required.</li>
          <li>The money did not refuse Canada. Most Canadian infrastructure was never offered to it: airports sit with not-for-profit authorities, water with municipalities, and a rule Ottawa promised to remove in 2024 was still in force this spring.</li>
          <li>Nobody here wants the water sold, and nothing in this paper sells it. The proposal is the other way round: the pipes stay public, and Canadians’ own retirement savings lend to them, at a return that keeps pace with prices.</li>
          <li>Three things Canada can do this year are at the end.</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <h3>How to read this</h3>
      <p>Every figure is taken from the funds’ own annual reports, Statistics Canada, the federal budget documents and the auditors who checked them, and each names the date it was true. The eight funds close their years three months apart, so their totals are given as “about”. Every source is at the end.</p>
      <p>It is written for the people who could move it: a council deciding how to fund its pipes, a provincial ministry setting up a water corporation, a pension investment committee, Finance Canada, and any Canadian who pays into a plan.</p>
      <p class="byline">Northern Temper. Drafted with the research in the open, 1 November 2026. <a href="#sources">The receipts are at the end.</a></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">01 · The model</span> Canada built it.</h2>
      <p>In 1990, Ontario gave its teachers’ pension plan an arm’s-length board and told it to invest like an owner. Until then the plan’s money sat in Ontario government debentures that could not be traded. Seven years later, Parliament did the same for the Canada Pension Plan.<sup><a href="#note-1">1</a>, <a href="#note-2">2</a></sup></p>
      <p>The law that created CPP Investments gives it one job: “to invest its assets with a view to achieving a maximum rate of return, without undue risk of loss.” Changing that law takes Ottawa plus two-thirds of the provinces holding two-thirds of the population. CPP Investments points out that this is a higher bar than amending the Constitution.<sup><a href="#note-2">2</a>, <a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></p>
      <p>The result became known abroad as the Canadian model. The funds hire their own investment teams, pay them to stay, and buy real things outright: ports, power lines, toll roads, buildings. In 2017 the World Bank published a study to teach it to other countries. Its summary: “Independent investment decision-making is essential.”<sup><a href="#note-4">4</a></sup></p>
      <dl class="facts">
        <div><dt>$2.6T</dt><dd>About what Canada’s eight largest public pension funds hold, summing each one’s latest year-end, 31 December 2025 or 31 March 2026.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>$793.3B</dt><dd>CPP Investments alone, at 31 March 2026, with $119.2 billion of it invested in Canada, “an all-time high in dollar terms”.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></dd></div>
        <div><dt>2.9%</dt><dd>Canada’s share of world stock markets, by the MSCI all-country index, 30 September 2026.<sup><a href="#note-6">6</a></sup></dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p>That last number matters, and it cuts against the easy version of this argument. Measured against the size of Canada’s stock market, the funds are already heavily invested at home: by their own reports, between about 12% and 49% of each fund is in Canada.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup> The gap this paper is about is narrower, and harder to see. It is the roads, pipes and power that Canadians use every day.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">02 · Where the model works</span> Ports in Britain. Airports in Europe. Toll roads in Sydney.</h2>
      <p>The funds are very good at owning infrastructure. Most of what they own is somewhere else.</p>
      <section class="tw" tabindex="0" aria-label="Figure 1, infrastructure owned by Canadian pension funds, scrolls sideways">
        <table class="keyed">
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Fund</th> <th scope="col">Abroad</th> <th scope="col">At home</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">CPP Investments</th> <td>33.9% of Associated British Ports, Britain’s largest port operator, and Sydney toll roads, both agreed for sale in October 2026.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a>, <a href="#note-8">8</a></sup></td> <td>Highway 407 ETR in Ontario, majority stake since 2019.<sup><a href="#note-9">9</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">La Caisse (formerly CDPQ)</th> <td>The Port of Brisbane, the New South Wales transmission grid, Eurostar, a share of Britain’s Sizewell C nuclear plant.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></td> <td>The REM, Montréal’s automated light rail, which it built and owns.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Ontario Teachers’</th> <td>Five European airports, among them Brussels, Copenhagen and London City, owned from 2001 until their sale in 2025.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></td> <td>About $100 billion of its gross assets are in Canada.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">PSP Investments</th> <td>AviAlliance, which owns or holds stakes in the airports of Athens, Düsseldorf, Hamburg, San Juan, Aberdeen, Glasgow and Southampton.<sup><a href="#note-10">10</a></sup></td> <td>A share of Highway 407 ETR.<sup><a href="#note-9">9</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">OMERS</th> <td>About 32% of the parent of Thames Water in London, written off in full.<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></td> <td>A stake in Bruce Power, which supplies about 30% of Ontario’s electricity.<sup><a href="#note-12">12</a></sup></td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">BCI</th> <td>An infrastructure portfolio of $36.5 billion, gross, with renewable resources.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></td> <td>Container terminals on the West Coast, the Golden Ears Bridge, and the Canada Line SkyTrain under a partnership contract.<sup><a href="#note-5">5</a></sup></td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </section>
      <p class="fig">Figure 1 · What six of the eight funds own, from their own reports and releases. Not a complete list.</p>
      <p>Australia learned from the same model and did one thing differently: it brought it home. Its retirement funds led the purchase of Sydney Airport, which stays majority Australian owned, and hold half the 99-year lease on Ausgrid, Sydney’s electricity network. In October 2026, Australian-owned capital agreed to buy the British ports that CPP Investments and OMERS were selling.<sup><a href="#note-7">7</a>, <a href="#note-13">13</a></sup></p>
      <BarTable name="Figure 2" rowHead="Pension funds of" columns={['Share of infrastructure held at home']} unit="percent" rows={[
        { label: 'Australia', values: [46] },
        { label: 'Canada', values: [7] },
      ]}>Figure 2 · The share of each country’s pension infrastructure held at home, from one study by the Global Risk Institute, 2024. The same study found Canadian funds keep 57% of their real estate at home.<sup><a href="#note-14">14</a></sup></BarTable>
      <p class="pull">Canada taught the world to own its infrastructure with its retirement savings. Australia was the student that brought it home.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">03 · Under our streets</span> The water is the biggest asset Canada owns, and it is wearing out.</h2>
      <p>On 30 October 2026, Statistics Canada published its newest count of the country’s public infrastructure. At the end of 2024, local and regional governments held more than $2.0 trillion of it. More than half of that value, 52%, is water: drinking water, wastewater and stormwater.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></p>
      <p>It is almost all local. Of 200,520 kilometres of drinking-water pipe in the survey, 199,220 kilometres belong to local and regional governments. The same is true of the wastewater pipe.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></p>
      <p>And it is getting worse. In 2024, municipal water assets worth $124.3 billion to replace were in poor or very poor condition, 14% of the total and 3 percentage points more than in 2022. “Very poor” is Statistics Canada’s term for an asset where “health and safety hazards exist”.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></p>
      <BarTable name="Figure 3" rowHead="Municipal asset" columns={['Poor or very poor, share of value']} unit="percent" rows={[
        { label: 'Public transit', values: [18] },
        { label: 'Roads', values: [18] },
        { label: 'Drinking water', values: [17] },
        { label: 'Wastewater', values: [16] },
        { label: 'Stormwater', values: [9] },
        { label: 'Bridges and tunnels', values: [9] },
      ]}>Figure 3 · Municipal assets in poor or very poor condition at the end of 2024, as a share of replacement value. Each row adds Statistics Canada’s two rounded shares.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></BarTable>
      <p>The repair bill is not being paid at the rate it grows. At the end of 2022, the owners of Canada’s core public infrastructure estimated that $294.4 billion was needed to rehabilitate or replace what they had. That year they put $31.3 billion into renewal, 11% of it.<sup><a href="#note-16">16</a></sup></p>
      <p>Ottawa built a bank for this, the Canada Infrastructure Bank. By March 2025 it had invested $15.8 billion in 94 projects. Of that, $247 million was in water and wastewater.<sup><a href="#note-17">17</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">04 · Why the money went abroad</span> It was never offered the pipes.</h2>
      <p>The easy story is that the funds chose London over Lethbridge. The evidence says something else. The 2024 study that found the 7% put the barrier on the supply side: few Canadian public assets are open to investment, and permits take an average of 249 days here against 80 in the United States. Its authors warned against forcing the funds to invest at home.<sup><a href="#note-14">14</a></sup></p>
      <p>Look at what is for sale. Canada’s big airports are run by not-for-profit authorities on federal ground leases. Water belongs to municipalities. In December 2024, Ottawa said it would look at changing the airport leases, at lowering the 90 per cent threshold that limits municipally owned utility corporations, and at removing a rule that stops a pension plan owning more than 30% of the votes of a Canadian company.<sup><a href="#note-18">18</a></sup></p>
      <p>The 30% rule was still in the federal regulation at 31 March 2026, word for word.<sup><a href="#note-19">19</a></sup></p>
      <p>Since then the money has moved. In April 2026 Ottawa launched the Canada Strong Fund, a $25 billion sovereign wealth fund. In September, CPP Investments and Brookfield launched a $50 billion fund for Canadian infrastructure and industry, and Ontario Teachers’ set out an additional $10 billion for Canada by the end of 2027. OMERS has committed at least $10 billion over five years.<sup><a href="#note-20">20</a></sup></p>
      <p>Not one of those announcements names a water pipe. The funds invest in what they can buy, and the largest public asset in the country is not on the list.</p>
      <p class="pull">The money did not refuse Canada. Nobody offered it the pipes.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">05 · The worry</span> Canadians want the water public. So does this paper.</h2>
      <p>The first answer to any proposal like this is “privatization”, and it deserves a straight reply. Every poll found on the question says Canadians want water in public hands: three-quarters in a national survey in 2001, three-quarters of British Columbians in 2005. All of those polls were commissioned by a public-sector union, and no independent national poll was found.<sup><a href="#note-21">21</a></sup></p>
      <p>Ontario has already written the public answer into law. Its 2025 act lets the province move a region’s water into a new public corporation, starting with Peel, and the government has committed that the corporation will stay publicly owned. A bill this spring proposed making that 100% public ownership a legal requirement.<sup><a href="#note-22">22</a></sup></p>
      <p>The record of foreign ownership is the best argument for keeping it that way. OMERS owned about 32% of the parent of Thames Water, London’s water company, and wrote off a holding once worth about $1 billion. Canadian retirement money’s stake in London’s water ended as a total loss.<sup><a href="#note-11">11</a></sup></p>
      <p>The REM shows the other lesson. La Caisse built Montréal’s new light rail and owns it, and the project’s cost grew from $6.3 billion financed in 2018 to $8.34 billion. In May 2026 its leadership told the National Assembly that it would build the REM differently today, and leave running it to a local operator.<sup><a href="#note-23">23</a></sup></p>
      <p>There is a model already working that keeps both lessons. EPCOR is owned by the City of Edmonton, pays the city a dividend, and builds, finances and runs a wastewater plant for Regina under a 30-year partnership that Regina’s voters chose in 2013.<sup><a href="#note-24">24</a></sup> Public ownership and outside capital are not opposites. The question is only who holds the deed.</p>
      <p>Here is the part that changes the argument. When the Canada Infrastructure Bank told Parliament in 2022 that its pension fund partners were private money, the Parliamentary Budget Officer disagreed. Under Statistics Canada’s definitions, his office counts public pension funds as “government entities”.<sup><a href="#note-25">25</a></sup></p>
      <p class="view"><strong>Our view:</strong> a public pension fund lending to a public water corporation is not privatization. It is Canadians lending to themselves, through teachers’, nurses’, municipal workers’ and federal employees’ plans and the CPP, with the pipes kept in public hands. The model that is correct to fear is a foreign owner holding the deed. The model that is correct to build is the deed staying with the city and the savings staying in Canada.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">06 · The bond</span> Long, slow and tied to prices: what a water pipe pays, and what a pension needs.</h2>
      <p>A pension has to pay people for decades, in dollars that inflation will shrink. The asset that fits it best pays a steady return for decades that rises with prices. That is what infrastructure pays, and it is why the funds own so much of it.<sup><a href="#note-26">26</a></sup></p>
      <p>Canada used to sell exactly that kind of bond. In November 2022, Ottawa stopped issuing Real Return Bonds, its inflation-linked bonds, citing “low demand for this product”. The actuaries asked it to reconsider, the Bank of Canada’s fixed-income advisory forum unanimously agreed it should, and a survey of 13 large holders in 2024 found not one that supported the decision.<sup><a href="#note-27">27</a></sup></p>
      <p>Five years earlier, Ottawa had stopped selling Canada Savings Bonds, the bond ordinary Canadians bought at the bank, also for declining demand.<sup><a href="#note-27">27</a></sup> In April 2026 it announced a retail product for the Canada Strong Fund, in which “initial invested capital will be protected”. Its design has not been published.<sup><a href="#note-20">20</a></sup></p>
