      <p class="view"><strong>Our view:</strong> the bond that fits a water pipe is the bond Canada stopped selling. Bring it back, and let the water corporations issue it. The retirement savings of the people who drink the water then pay for the pipes, and are paid back by the same water rates, in dollars that keep their value.</p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">07 · The case against</span> Three objections, taken seriously.</h2>
      <p><strong>Diversification.</strong> A Canadian pension already depends on Canadian jobs. Putting more of its money in Canada concentrates that risk. CPP Investments says so plainly: it diversifies “across the world” to protect the fund from risks to Canada’s economy.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup> That is why this paper asks for lending against public assets, with returns tied to prices, and not for a quota.</p>
      <p><strong>Fiduciary duty.</strong> The law requires these funds to act for their members, not for a government. A policy that pushed them into poor investments would break that, and South Korea shows what happens: a former minister was jailed for pressuring the national pension fund to back a merger that hurt it.<sup><a href="#note-28">28</a>, <a href="#note-29">29</a></sup> The proposal here works only if the bond earns a fair return on its own merits.</p>
      <p><strong>Returns at home.</strong> The evidence is mixed, not one-sided. Canada’s stock market lagged the American one over the two decades to 2025. But a 2026 study found Canadian funds did better at home than abroad in private equity and venture capital.<sup><a href="#note-30">30</a></sup> Infrastructure, priced fairly, is closer to the second case than the first.</p>
      <p class="view"><strong>Our view:</strong> every one of these objections argues against forcing the money home. None of them argues against offering it something worth buying. That is a different policy, and it fits what the funds say of themselves: they invest at home “when opportunities meet our requirements”.<sup><a href="#note-3">3</a></sup></p>

      <h2><span class="eyebrow">08 · What to do</span> Three things Canada can do this year.</h2>
      <ol class="asks">
        <li><h3>Finish the rule Ottawa already announced.</h3><p>The removal of the 30% rule was promised in December 2024 and was still in the regulation this spring. Amend it, and publish the airport-lease and utility-threshold reviews announced the same day, so the funds know what is on offer.<sup><a href="#note-18">18</a>, <a href="#note-19">19</a></sup></p></li>
        <li><h3>Let public water corporations borrow from Canadians.</h3><p>Provinces setting up water corporations, as Ontario has, can give them the power to issue long bonds whose payments rise with inflation, with the deed held publicly by law. The Canada Infrastructure Bank already lends to municipal water systems that stay publicly owned, as in Manitoba, and can lend alongside the pensions.<sup><a href="#note-17">17</a>, <a href="#note-22">22</a></sup></p></li>
        <li><h3>Bring back the Real Return Bond.</h3><p>Resume federal inflation-linked bonds, as the actuaries and the bond market’s own advisory forum asked. And design the Canada Strong Fund’s retail product so that any Canadian can hold a piece of the country’s renewed infrastructure, the way their pension already could.<sup><a href="#note-20">20</a>, <a href="#note-27">27</a></sup></p></li>
      </ol>
      <p class="view"><strong>Our view:</strong> Canada does not have to choose between keeping its water public and paying to renew it. It invented the way the world’s savings own infrastructure. It can use that invention on its own streets, with the deed staying with the people who drink the water.</p>

      <p class="pull">Canada built the model. Bring it home, and keep the deed.</p>
