      <h2 id="words"><span class="eyebrow">Words used here</span> Seven terms, plainly.</h2>
      <dl>
        <dt>The Canadian model</dt>
        <dd>A way of running a public pension plan: an arm’s-length board, an in-house investment team, and direct ownership of real assets rather than only stocks and bonds.<sup><a href="#note-4">4</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Maple 8</dt>
        <dd>An informal name for Canada’s eight largest public pension investors: CPP Investments, La Caisse, Ontario Teachers’, PSP Investments, OMERS, BCI, AIMCo and HOOPP.</dd>
        <dt>Infrastructure</dt>
        <dd>The physical systems a country runs on: water and sewer pipes, roads, bridges, ports, airports, transit, power lines.</dd>
        <dt>Replacement value</dt>
        <dd>What it would cost to replace an asset today, which is how Statistics Canada measures the size of public infrastructure.<sup><a href="#note-15">15</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Real Return Bond</dt>
        <dd>A federal bond whose payments rise with the consumer price index, so the lender is repaid in dollars that keep their value. Ottawa stopped issuing them in 2022.<sup><a href="#note-27">27</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>The 30% rule</dt>
        <dd>A federal pension regulation that stops a plan owning more than 30% of the votes that elect a company’s board.<sup><a href="#note-19">19</a></sup></dd>
        <dt>Fiduciary duty</dt>
        <dd>The legal obligation of a fund to act in the interest of its members, with the care of a prudent person handling someone else’s property.<sup><a href="#note-29">29</a></sup></dd>
      </dl>

